      "content": "Thanks for that breakdown. One thing that's making this decision difficult for our team is the sheer irregularity of the items. We have a lot of \"orphaned\" fragments—pieces of letters, loose notes, and scraps that don't fit into standard subject headings. \n\nWhen we encounter these kinds of chaotic, miscellany-heavy bundles that seem to defy logical categorization, how does LCC handle the risk of them becoming \"lost\" or hidden within the system compared to the more rigid Dewey approach? Is there a way to prevent these disorganized elements from essentially breaking the classification logic?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
